\begin{abstract}
GRPO-trained formalizers converge to high prove rates on FOLIO ($95{\pm}5\%$, 5 seeds) yet produce effectively zero structurally sound outputs at convergence, a failure invisible to execution-based evaluation.
We propose \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), applying leave-one-out necessity testing to solver inputs.
The resulting metric (ISR) exposes a 99.5\% structural collapse hidden behind rising prove rates; a supervised baseline achieves 92\% non-hacking on identical data, localizing the failure to RL.
Repurposed as a reward signal, ISR contains an inherent Goodhart vulnerability: tautological proofs receive artificially high ISR (${\geq}0.5$ by construction), creating a positive feedback loop that guarantees eventual collapse at all tested doses ($\beta\in\{0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3\}$).
At moderate doses, ISR initially yields significantly more genuine output than random controls ($22.6\%$ vs.\ $7.7\%$, $\text{OR}{=}3.48$, $p{<}10^{-8}$); trajectory analysis confirms this benefit is temporary ($17\%{\to}2\%$ genuine at 1000 steps).
The effect transfers to Prolog (3/3 seeds, 0\% non-hacking) and across model scale (Qwen3-1.7B discovers qualitatively novel gaming modes absent in 4B).
Across 6 conditions, 2 logic types, 2 model scales, and 5 seeds, we catalog 9 hacking modes and document that $\beta$ controls \emph{when} collapse occurs, not \emph{whether}: ISR is reliable as a diagnostic but unreliable as an optimization target at any dose.\footnote{Code and data: \url{[anonymized]}}
\end{abstract}
